# Reorganising Linux Infrastructure for Long-Term Maintainability

## Overview

As the project grew, the backend application was still organised around an early development workflow where application files lived inside a personal user directory.

While this worked initially, it made administration, collaboration and future automation increasingly difficult.

The objective was to reorganise the server into a cleaner, service-oriented structure that followed common Linux administration practices without affecting application availability.

---

## The Challenge

The existing layout introduced several operational problems:

- Application files were tied to a specific user account.
- Ownership and permissions became harder to manage.
- Multiple administrators required access to the same project.
- Future deployment automation would depend on a personal directory structure.
- The server organisation no longer reflected the growing production environment.

Although the application continued to function correctly, the infrastructure itself was becoming harder to maintain.

---

## My Approach

Instead of leaving the historical layout in place, I reorganised the infrastructure to better separate application services from personal user data.

The work included:

- relocating the application into a dedicated service location
- reviewing file ownership and permissions
- simplifying administrator access
- removing dependency on an individual user's home directory
- preparing the project for automated deployments and future team growth

The objective was not only to improve today's server structure but also to reduce operational complexity as the project expands.

---

